-- Q1: hard / L4 — Zero-cost collar on own shares (written put + purchased call)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgemont Corp. enters into a zero-cost collar on 30,000 of its own shares, consisting of a written put at $40 and a purchased call at $70. During 2025, the average market price is $35. For diluted EPS, evaluate the net treatment of the collar under ASC 260-10-45-35.', '["Both legs are excluded because the collar is a single hedging instrument", "The written put at $40 is dilutive and included via the reverse treasury stock method; the purchased call at $70 is out of the money and excluded", "Both legs are included because any derivative on own shares affects diluted EPS regardless of moneyness", "The purchased call at $70 offsets the written put, so the collar has zero net dilutive effect"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, each leg of a collar is evaluated separately. The written put at $40 is in the money because the exercise price ($40) exceeds the average market price ($35) — the counterparty would exercise, forcing Ridgemont to repurchase shares above market value. Under the reverse treasury stock method, the assumed repurchase of 30,000 shares at $40 funded by issuing shares at $35 creates incremental dilutive shares. The purchased call at $70 is out of the money (exercise price > market price) and is excluded as antidilutive. Choice D is wrong because ASC 260 does not allow netting of separate derivative legs for EPS purposes.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — ATM equity offering continuous issuance weighting
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Wavecrest Inc. conducts an at-the-market (ATM) equity offering throughout 2025, issuing shares continuously. Shares sold: 6,000 in February, 9,000 in May, 12,000 in August, and 3,000 in November. Before the ATM program, Wavecrest had 500,000 shares outstanding on January 1. What methodology determines how ATM shares enter the weighted-average denominator for basic EPS?', '["All 30,000 ATM shares are included for the full year because the program was authorized on January 1", "Each tranche is weighted from its actual issuance date through year-end, the same as any other share issuance for consideration", "ATM shares are excluded until the offering program closes because the total share count is uncertain", "ATM shares are treated like a stock dividend and applied retroactively to all periods"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued for consideration are included in the weighted-average from the date of issuance. Each ATM tranche enters the denominator from the specific date shares are delivered to buyers, weighted for the portion of the year outstanding. There is no special treatment for ATM programs — they follow standard issuance-date weighting. Choice A is wrong because authorization alone does not trigger inclusion. Choice D is incorrect because ATM issuances involve cash consideration, unlike stock dividends.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Convertible bonds issued at substantial premium
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Foxworth Corp. issued $5,000,000 face value of 3% convertible bonds at 112 (total proceeds $5,600,000). Under ASU 2020-06, the entire instrument is recorded as a single liability. Effective interest expense is $190,000 annually. For diluted EPS using the if-converted method, what amount is added back to the numerator?', '["$150,000 — the coupon interest, net of tax", "$190,000 — the effective interest, before tax", "The after-tax effective interest expense recognized under the effective interest method", "The after-tax coupon payment of $150,000 because that represents the cash outflow avoided"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method adds back the after-tax interest expense that would be eliminated if conversion had occurred. When bonds are issued at a premium, the recognized interest expense under the effective interest method differs from the coupon. The correct add-back is the after-tax effective interest expense actually recognized, not the coupon amount. Choice D is wrong because EPS adjustments are based on recognized expense, not cash payments.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Restricted stock with forfeitable dividends is NOT participating
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Granton Corp. grants 20,000 unvested restricted shares to executives. The award agreement states that dividends accumulate during the vesting period but are forfeited if the employee terminates before vesting. Net income is $1,200,000 with 280,000 common shares outstanding. How should these unvested shares be treated for basic EPS under ASC 260?', '["As participating securities under the two-class method because they receive dividends", "As contingently issuable shares included in both basic and diluted EPS", "They are excluded from basic EPS because the forfeitable dividend rights mean they are not participating securities", "They are included in the basic EPS denominator at 280,000 plus 20,000 shares"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-59A, only awards with nonforfeitable dividend rights qualify as participating securities. When dividends are forfeitable upon termination, the awards do not meet the participation threshold and are excluded from the two-class method for basic EPS. They may still be included in diluted EPS via the treasury stock method. Choice A is wrong because forfeitability of dividends is the key distinguishing factor under the guidance.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Simple capital structure defined
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ferndale Corp. has only common stock and nonconvertible, nonparticipating preferred stock outstanding. The company has no options, warrants, or other potentially dilutive instruments. Under ASC 260, what type of capital structure does Ferndale have, and what EPS presentation is required?', '["Complex structure — both basic and diluted EPS required", "Simple structure — only basic EPS is required on the face of the income statement", "Simple structure — but both basic and diluted EPS must still be presented", "Complex structure — because the existence of preferred stock always creates complexity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-1 through 45-3, an entity has a simple capital structure when it has no outstanding securities or agreements that could potentially dilute EPS. Nonconvertible, nonparticipating preferred stock is not a potentially dilutive security. Entities with simple structures present only basic EPS. Choice D is wrong because preferred stock creates a complex structure only when it is convertible into common shares or otherwise potentially dilutive.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Shares issued for services weighted from issuance date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On September 1, 2025, Ashvale Corp. issues 8,000 common shares to a marketing firm as compensation for services rendered. Ashvale had 192,000 shares outstanding from January 1. For what fraction of the year are the 8,000 shares included in the weighted-average denominator for basic EPS?', '["The full year, because the services were rendered before issuance", "Four-twelfths (September through December)", "Zero — shares issued for services are excluded from basic EPS", "Eight-twelfths, retroactive to May when the services began"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued as compensation are included in the weighted-average from the issuance date, not from when services began or were completed. The 8,000 shares are outstanding from September 1 through December 31, contributing 4/12 of the year to the weighted-average. Choice A is wrong because shares do not enter the denominator until actually issued, regardless of when the related services occurred.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Excess tax benefits no longer affect treasury stock method assumed proceeds post-ASU 2016-09
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Prior to ASU 2016-09, entities included excess tax benefits from share-based payments as part of assumed proceeds in the treasury stock method for diluted EPS. After the adoption of ASU 2016-09, how does the treatment of excess tax benefits in the treasury stock method change?', '["Excess tax benefits are still included in assumed proceeds because the EPS guidance was not amended", "Excess tax benefits are excluded from assumed proceeds because they are now recognized in income tax expense rather than additional paid-in capital", "Excess tax benefits increase the numerator instead of being part of assumed proceeds", "The treasury stock method was replaced by the if-converted method for all stock-based awards"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASU 2016-09 (ASC 718-740) requires all excess tax benefits and deficiencies to be recognized in income tax expense rather than APIC. Because the windfall tax benefit is no longer credited to APIC upon assumed exercise, ASC 260-10-45-29 was amended to remove it from the assumed proceeds in the treasury stock method. Choice A is incorrect because ASU 2016-09 specifically amended the EPS guidance to conform with the new income statement treatment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Spin-off retroactive EPS adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On October 1, 2025, Hartsfield Corp. completes a tax-free spin-off of its chemical division, distributing 1 share of the new entity for every 4 shares of Hartsfield common stock held. Hartsfield had 400,000 shares outstanding before the spin-off. When presenting comparative EPS for 2024, how must Hartsfield adjust the prior-year weighted-average shares?', '["No adjustment — the spin-off distribution does not change the parent''s share count", "The 2024 weighted-average is reduced by the 100,000 distributed shares", "The 2024 weighted-average is retroactively adjusted using the spin-off distribution ratio to maintain per-share comparability, similar to a stock dividend", "The 2024 EPS is restated by removing the chemical division''s contribution from the numerator only"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260, a spin-off does not change the number of parent shares outstanding, unlike a stock dividend or split. The parent''s share count remains 400,000 after the spin-off because the distributed shares are shares of a new entity, not additional parent shares. The parent simply removes the spun-off subsidiary''s results from the numerator for comparative purposes if it meets discontinued operations criteria. Choice C is wrong because the distribution ratio does not alter the parent''s own share count.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Step-up conversion ratio on convertible preferred
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Kessington Corp. has 10,000 shares of convertible preferred stock. The conversion terms specify that if converted before December 31, 2025, each share converts into 3 common shares; if converted after that date, each share converts into 4 common shares due to an anti-dilution step-up. No conversions have occurred as of year-end 2025. For diluted EPS in 2025, evaluate which conversion ratio should be used in the if-converted method.', '["The 3-share ratio because that was the applicable rate during the reporting period", "The 4-share ratio because the most dilutive terms available must be used", "The higher ratio of 4 shares per preferred, because ASC 260 requires using the conversion terms that would produce the maximum number of potential common shares", "The weighted-average of the two ratios based on when each was in effect"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method assumes conversion at the beginning of the period (or issuance date, if later) using the conversion terms in effect during the period. During 2025, the conversion ratio was 3 shares per preferred share. The step-up to 4 shares takes effect only after December 31, 2025, so it is not applicable to the 2025 computation. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 260 uses the terms in effect, not the most dilutive possible future terms.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: hard / L3 — Contract indexed to peer company stock is not a potential common share
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Blackwood Corp. issues 5,000 instruments that entitle the holder to receive Blackwood common shares, but the number of shares delivered is determined by the stock price of an unrelated peer company. If the peer''s stock exceeds $100, the holder receives 10 Blackwood shares per instrument; otherwise, 5 shares. The peer''s average price during 2025 is $85. Analyze whether these instruments should be included in Blackwood''s diluted EPS.', '["Included at 5 shares per instrument because the $85 price triggers the lower tier", "Excluded entirely because instruments whose settlement is indexed to a third party''s stock price are not potential common shares of the reporting entity under ASC 260", "Included at 10 shares per instrument using the most dilutive assumption", "Included as contingently issuable shares at the 5-share level because the condition is currently met"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in basic EPS when all conditions are met and in diluted EPS when the conditions are currently satisfied. Here, the condition (peer price) is met at the 5-share tier. These are Blackwood shares indexed to an external condition, which makes them contingently issuable — not excluded. Choice B is incorrect because the instruments settle in Blackwood''s own shares; the external index is merely the contingency trigger.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — Redeemable NCI and consolidated EPS numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Edgefield Holdings owns 70% of a subsidiary. The subsidiary''s 30% noncontrolling interest is redeemable at the holders'' option at fair value, classified in mezzanine equity under ASC 480-10-S99. In 2025, the redemption value increases by $200,000 beyond the NCI''s share of earnings. Edgefield''s consolidated net income attributable to the parent is $3,000,000 with 500,000 shares. Evaluate how the $200,000 remeasurement affects consolidated basic EPS.', '["It has no effect because the adjustment is within equity, not income", "The $200,000 is added to income available to common shareholders because it increases the NCI balance", "The $200,000 remeasurement to redemption value reduces income available to common shareholders, similar to a preferred dividend adjustment", "The adjustment is excluded from EPS because it relates to a subsidiary, not the parent entity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under SEC Staff guidance (ASC 480-10-S99-3A), when a redeemable NCI is adjusted to its redemption value and that amount exceeds the NCI''s carrying amount from earnings, the excess adjustment is treated as a reduction of income available to common shareholders for EPS, analogous to a preferred dividend. The $200,000 decreases the EPS numerator. Choice A is wrong because the remeasurement, while recorded in equity, has a direct EPS impact per SEC guidance.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Weighted-average concept for partial-year shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Sunridge Corp. had 100,000 shares outstanding for the first six months of 2025 and 140,000 shares outstanding for the last six months after issuing 40,000 shares on July 1. Net income for 2025 is $600,000. Under ASC 260, basic EPS uses the weighted-average shares, not the ending share count. What is the weighted-average and basic EPS?', '["140,000 shares; EPS = $4.29", "120,000 shares; EPS = $5.00", "100,000 shares; EPS = $6.00", "240,000 shares; EPS = $2.50"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares are weighted by the fraction of the period they were outstanding. Weighted-average = (100,000 x 6/12) + (140,000 x 6/12) = 50,000 + 70,000 = 120,000. Basic EPS = $600,000 / 120,000 = $5.00. Choice A uses only ending shares without weighting. Choice C uses only beginning shares. The time-weighting ensures EPS reflects the capital available to generate earnings throughout the period.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Entity settles obligation by delivering own shares: effect on EPS depends on classification
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Meridian Dynamics has a $900,000 liability that the contract allows it to settle by delivering its own common shares at their then-current market value. The company elects share settlement and issues 18,000 shares (market price $50 each) on August 1 to extinguish the obligation. Analyze how the share issuance and liability derecognition interact with the EPS computation.', '["The 18,000 shares are excluded from EPS because they were issued to settle a liability rather than raise capital", "The 18,000 shares enter the weighted-average denominator from August 1 like any issuance, and the $900,000 liability reversal is already reflected in net income if the settlement created a gain or loss", "The shares reduce the EPS numerator by the $900,000 settlement cost", "The shares are included in diluted EPS only because the settlement was optional"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued are included in the weighted-average from the issuance date regardless of purpose. The 18,000 shares enter the denominator from August 1. The liability derecognition removes the obligation from the balance sheet; any gain or loss on settlement is already in net income (the numerator). Before settlement, the contract would have been evaluated for diluted EPS inclusion under ASC 260-10-45-45. Choice A is wrong because the reason for issuance does not gate inclusion in the weighted-average.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Contingent consideration requiring variable share issuance
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'In a business combination, Grayfield Corp. agreed to pay contingent consideration by issuing enough shares to deliver $2,000,000 of value if the target''s Year 2 revenue exceeds $10 million. In Year 1, the target''s revenue is $11 million, making the condition probable. Grayfield''s average share price is $50. Evaluate the diluted EPS treatment of this contingent share obligation in Year 1.', '["The contingent shares are excluded until Year 2 because the measurement year has not arrived", "Include 40,000 shares (2,000,000 / 50) in diluted EPS because the contingency condition is currently met based on Year 1 performance", "Include the shares only in basic EPS since the condition has been satisfied", "Exclude the shares because the number is variable and cannot be determined until settlement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in diluted EPS if the conditions are currently met (as if the end of the reporting period were the end of the contingency period). Since Year 1 revenue exceeds $10 million, the condition is met. The number of shares is determinable using the current share price ($2,000,000 / $50 = 40,000). Choice A is wrong because the "currently met" test evaluates conditions as of the reporting date, not the contractual measurement date.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Change in accounting estimate does not require EPS restatement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'In 2025, Langford Corp. revises the useful life of a major asset class from 10 years to 7 years, increasing depreciation expense by $150,000 prospectively. The controller asks whether the 2024 comparative EPS must be restated for the change. Under ASC 250 and ASC 260, what is the correct treatment?', '["Restate 2024 EPS to reflect the revised depreciation as if it had always been used", "Restate 2024 EPS only if the change is material to the financial statements", "No restatement of prior-period EPS — a change in accounting estimate is applied prospectively and does not affect previously reported EPS", "Restate 2024 EPS for the cumulative catch-up effect of the change"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-17, changes in accounting estimates are applied prospectively. Prior-period financial statements, including EPS, are not restated. The increased depreciation affects only current and future periods. This contrasts with changes in accounting principles and error corrections, which may require retrospective restatement of prior EPS. Choice A incorrectly applies retrospective treatment reserved for changes in accounting principles.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — ESOP suspense account shares excluded until committed to be released
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hartsfield Corp. sponsors a leveraged ESOP that holds 100,000 shares in a suspense account. During 2025, 20,000 of those shares are committed to be released as employees earn compensation. Hartsfield has 500,000 other shares outstanding and net income of $2,500,000. For basic EPS, how are the ESOP shares treated?', '["All 100,000 ESOP shares are included because they are legally issued", "None of the ESOP shares are included because the ESOP is a separate legal entity", "Only the 20,000 shares committed to be released are included in the weighted-average; the 80,000 suspense shares are excluded", "All 100,000 shares are excluded until the ESOP loan is fully repaid"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 718-40-45-4 and ASC 260, shares held in an ESOP suspense account that have not been committed to be released are not considered outstanding for EPS purposes. Only the 20,000 shares committed to be released during 2025 are included in the weighted-average denominator, weighted from the date of commitment. The remaining 80,000 suspense shares are excluded. Choice A is wrong because legal issuance to the ESOP trust does not make the shares outstanding for EPS.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Individual dilution test before aggregate: each security tested separately
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Wellspring Corp. has basic EPS of $6.00 ($1,200,000 income, 200,000 shares) and two convertible instruments. Bond A has a per-share impact of $3.00 (dilutive individually). Bond B has a per-share impact of $8.00 (antidilutive individually). If tested together in aggregate, the combined per-share impact would be $5.50 (apparently dilutive). Should both bonds be included in diluted EPS?', '["Yes — the aggregate test shows a dilutive combined impact of $5.50 versus basic EPS of $6.00", "No — only Bond A is included because each security must be tested individually in dilutive sequence, and Bond B is antidilutive", "Yes — both bonds must be included because antidilutive securities are excluded only when no dilutive securities exist", "No — both bonds are excluded because the presence of an antidilutive security taints the entire group"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, potentially dilutive securities are ranked from most dilutive to least dilutive and tested sequentially. Bond B, with a per-share impact of $8.00 (exceeding running cumulative EPS after Bond A), is antidilutive at that step and excluded. Choice A is wrong because ASC 260 does not permit aggregating securities to mask an individually antidilutive instrument. The sequential ranking-and-testing approach prevents this.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Shares held by consolidated VIE included in denominator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Whitmore Holdings consolidates a variable interest entity (VIE) that holds 15,000 shares of Whitmore''s common stock as an investment. Whitmore has 500,000 total shares issued, of which the VIE holds the 15,000. What is the correct treatment of these 15,000 shares for basic EPS purposes?', '["Excluded from the denominator, similar to treasury shares, because the consolidated entity effectively holds them", "Included in the denominator because they are legally outstanding shares held by an outside entity", "Excluded from basic EPS but included in diluted EPS", "Included at 50% of their balance because the VIE is only partially consolidated"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10 and ASC 810, when a reporting entity consolidates a VIE that holds the parent''s shares, those shares are treated as treasury stock in the consolidated financial statements and excluded from the weighted-average denominator. The consolidation eliminates the intercompany investment, making the shares equivalent to shares held in treasury. Choice B is wrong because consolidation principles override the legal form of the ownership.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — EPS voluntary disclosure by nonpublic entity must follow ASC 260
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A closely held technology firm with no publicly traded securities voluntarily includes EPS data in its audited annual financial statements provided to venture capital investors. The CFO proposes computing EPS using a simplified formula that excludes potential dilution from employee stock options. Under ASC 260-10-15-3, what constraint applies?', '["The company may compute EPS using any reasonable methodology since it is not required to present EPS", "If the company chooses to present EPS voluntarily, it must follow ASC 260 in its entirety, including diluted EPS for all potentially dilutive instruments", "The company need only present basic EPS and may omit diluted EPS because it is nonpublic", "The company may present a simplified EPS metric as long as it labels the figure clearly"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-3, any entity that voluntarily presents EPS must compute and present it in accordance with ASC 260, including both basic and diluted EPS when potentially dilutive securities exist. The entity cannot selectively apply parts of the standard. Choice A is wrong because once an entity elects to present EPS, the full standard applies regardless of its public or private status.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Year-to-date EPS for interim periods using cumulative average
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Dalworth Corp. reports the following quarterly data: Q1 income $200,000 with 100,000 shares; Q2 income $300,000 with 120,000 shares (20,000 issued April 1). The controller computes Q1 EPS as $2.00 and Q2 standalone EPS as $2.50, then averages them for six-month EPS of $2.25. Under ASC 260 and ASC 270, is this approach correct?', '["Yes — averaging quarterly EPS figures is the standard method for interim cumulative EPS", "No — six-month EPS must be computed using cumulative six-month income divided by the six-month weighted-average shares, not by averaging per-share amounts", "Yes — but only if the quarterly figures are rounded to two decimal places before averaging", "No — interim EPS is always equal to Q2 standalone EPS because it replaces the prior quarter"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45 and ASC 270-10-50, year-to-date EPS is computed by dividing cumulative year-to-date income by the year-to-date weighted-average shares. Averaging per-share amounts across quarters is mathematically incorrect when share counts change between periods. Correct: cumulative income = $500,000; YTD weighted-average = (100,000 x 3/6) + (120,000 x 3/6) = 110,000; YTD EPS = $4.55. Choice A produces the wrong result whenever the denominator varies across quarters.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Warrants granted to customer reduce revenue, not increase EPS assumed proceeds
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgevale Corp. grants a key customer warrants to purchase 20,000 common shares at $15 each as part of a supply agreement. Under ASC 606-10-32-25, the fair value of the warrants ($100,000) reduces transaction price. The average market price is $40. For diluted EPS, how are these customer warrants treated?', '["They are excluded from diluted EPS because they were not issued to employees or investors", "They are included via the treasury stock method just like any other outstanding warrant, producing incremental shares based on the $15 exercise price versus the $40 market price", "They are included via the if-converted method because they are linked to a revenue arrangement", "The $100,000 revenue reduction is added back to the EPS numerator to offset the warrant dilution"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, all outstanding options and warrants (regardless of the holder''s identity) are included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method when they are in the money. Incremental shares = 20,000 minus (20,000 x $15 / $40) = 12,500. The fact that the warrants reduce revenue under ASC 606 does not change their EPS treatment. The revenue reduction is already reflected in net income (the numerator). Choice A is wrong because the holder''s status as a customer does not exempt the warrants from EPS inclusion.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Antidilutive securities disclosed in notes
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Northvale Corp. has 50,000 stock options with an exercise price of $60 while the average market price during 2025 is $45. These options are out of the money and excluded from diluted EPS. Under ASC 260-10-50, what disclosure is required for these antidilutive securities?', '["No disclosure is required because they have no effect on EPS", "The options must be disclosed in the notes, including the number of shares and terms, because they could be dilutive in future periods", "The options must be presented as a separate line item on the income statement", "Only the total number of antidilutive shares need be disclosed, without terms or details"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-50-1(c), entities must disclose securities that were not included in the diluted EPS computation because they were antidilutive, along with a description of the terms. This disclosure alerts users that these instruments could become dilutive if market conditions change. Choice A is wrong because ASC 260 specifically requires this disclosure. Choice D understates the required detail.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Share-based awards with performance conditions not yet probable
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On January 1, 2025, Dalton Industries grants 30,000 performance-vesting stock options (exercise price $20) that vest only if the company achieves a 20% return on equity in 2025. The average market price is $50. As of December 31, 2025, management concludes it is not probable that the ROE target will be met. How are these options treated for diluted EPS?', '["Included using the treasury stock method because they are legally outstanding options", "Excluded from diluted EPS because the performance condition is not currently satisfied", "Included in basic EPS as contingently issuable shares", "Excluded from both basic and diluted EPS but treated as antidilutive for disclosure purposes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48 and 45-28A, share-based payment awards with performance conditions are included in diluted EPS only if the performance condition would be satisfied as of the reporting date (the "currently met" test). Since the ROE target is not met, the options are excluded. This differs from market conditions, which do not gate inclusion. Choice A is wrong because outstanding status alone does not control inclusion when a performance condition exists.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Equity method investee issues shares that dilute investor's interest
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Wexford Holdings owns 40% of Briarcliff Inc. (equity method investment). Briarcliff issues new shares to third parties at above book value, diluting Wexford''s ownership to 35%. The issuance increases Briarcliff''s total equity by $2,000,000, of which $200,000 represents the gain attributable to Wexford''s diluted interest. Evaluate how this dilution gain affects Wexford''s EPS.', '["The dilution gain is excluded from EPS because it is an unrealized intercompany transaction", "Wexford recognizes the $200,000 dilution gain in income (or equity, per policy election) and it flows through the EPS numerator accordingly", "The gain is recognized only in other comprehensive income and has no EPS effect", "Wexford adjusts only the denominator of its EPS calculation to reflect the lower ownership percentage"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 323-10-40-1, when an investee issues shares that change the investor''s ownership percentage, the investor treats the change as if it had sold a proportionate share of its investment. The resulting gain or loss is recognized in income (or equity, depending on policy election under SAB Topic 5.H). If recognized in income, it flows through the EPS numerator. Choice A is wrong because this is not an intercompany transaction — it involves third-party issuance by the investee.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — EPS denominator when share subscription has been partially paid
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Woodvale Corp. enters into a stock subscription agreement for 20,000 shares at $40 per share. Subscribers have paid 60% of the subscription price ($480,000 of $800,000). The shares have not been legally issued. Under ASC 260, how are these subscribed-but-unissued shares treated in basic EPS?', '["All 20,000 shares are included because the subscription contract is binding", "Only 12,000 shares (60% of 20,000) are included, proportional to the amount paid", "The shares are excluded from the denominator until they are legally issued and the full subscription price is received or the shares are delivered", "The shares are included in diluted EPS only, using the treasury stock method with subscription receivable as assumed proceeds"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, only shares that are actually outstanding are included in the basic EPS denominator. Subscribed shares that have not been issued are not outstanding, even if partial payment has been received. They are excluded until the shares are legally issued. Choice A is wrong because a binding contract does not equate to issuance. Choice B incorrectly applies a proportional approach that ASC 260 does not contemplate.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — EPS rounding convention for public entities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ashvale Corp. computes basic EPS as $3.14667 per share. The controller rounds this to $3.15 on the face of the income statement. A new staff accountant suggests rounding to the nearest dollar ($3.00) to simplify the presentation. Under SEC Regulation S-X and common practice, what is the minimum precision for EPS presentation?', '["EPS must be rounded to the nearest whole dollar", "EPS must be presented to the nearest one cent (two decimal places), so $3.15 is acceptable", "EPS may be presented with any number of decimal places at management''s discretion", "EPS must carry four decimal places to avoid rounding bias"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under SEC Regulation S-X Rule 11-02 and longstanding practice, public entities present EPS figures rounded to the nearest cent (two decimal places). While ASC 260 itself does not specify rounding, SEC registrants follow S-X conventions, and presenting to two decimal places is the established standard. Choice A understates the required precision, which could materially distort per-share amounts for high-volume shareholders.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Share-based awards: unamortized comp cost as assumed proceeds
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, when computing diluted EPS via the treasury stock method for employee stock options, assumed proceeds include more than just the exercise price. Which additional item is included in assumed proceeds?', '["The intrinsic value of the option at the grant date", "The average remaining unamortized compensation cost attributable to future services", "The total fair value of the option at the reporting date", "The dividends expected to be paid on the underlying shares during the vesting period"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, assumed proceeds include the exercise price plus the average unamortized compensation cost related to future services not yet recognized. This additional amount reflects the future compensation expense the entity would receive as services from employees, reducing the incremental dilutive shares. Choice A is wrong because intrinsic value at grant date is not part of the assumed proceeds concept. Choice C confuses fair value with unamortized cost.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: easy / L2 — Shares issued in settlement of lawsuit weighted from issuance date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On November 1, 2025, Lakefield Corp. settles a patent infringement lawsuit by issuing 15,000 newly authorized common shares to the plaintiff. Before the settlement, Lakefield had 285,000 shares outstanding since January 1. For the annual EPS computation, the 15,000 settlement shares affect the weighted-average denominator in what way?', '["They are excluded because shares issued to settle litigation are not considered issued for value", "They enter the weighted-average from January 1 because the lawsuit originated before the fiscal year", "They are weighted from November 1 through December 31, contributing 2/12 of a full share-year to the denominator", "They enter the denominator at year-end only if the settlement is deemed fair by the court"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares are included in the weighted-average from the date of issuance regardless of the reason for issuance. The settlement shares are issued on November 1 and outstanding for 2 months (2/12 of the year). The nature of the consideration (cash, services, or settling an obligation) does not change the weighting mechanics. Choice B is wrong because the lawsuit''s origination date has no bearing on when shares enter the EPS denominator.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Weighted-average not affected by cash dividends
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Millridge Corp. had 250,000 shares outstanding for the entire year and declared a $2.00 per share cash dividend on November 15, payable December 15 to holders of record on December 1. Under ASC 260, does the declaration of the cash dividend change the weighted-average number of shares used in the basic EPS denominator?', '["Yes — the dividend reduces shares outstanding because cash leaves the entity", "Yes — shares are reduced by the number of shares receiving dividends", "No — cash dividends do not change the number of shares outstanding and therefore have no effect on the weighted-average denominator", "No — but the dividend amount must be added back to the EPS numerator"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted-average denominator reflects only changes in the number of shares outstanding (issuances, repurchases, stock dividends, splits). Cash dividends reduce retained earnings and cash but do not alter the share count. The denominator remains 250,000 shares. Choice A is wrong because a cash distribution affects the balance sheet, not the number of shares. Choice D incorrectly suggests a numerator adjustment for cash dividends.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: hard / L3 — Liability-classified share-based award settled in shares still affects diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ironwood Corp. grants 15,000 stock appreciation rights (SARs) classified as liabilities under ASC 718 because the employee can elect cash settlement. The SARs have a strike price of $20 and the average market price is $60. Each SAR entitles the holder to the spread in either cash or shares at the employee''s election. Ironwood remeasures the liability each period. Analyze the diluted EPS treatment.', '["The SARs are excluded from diluted EPS because they are liability-classified instruments, not equity", "The SARs are included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method based on the number of shares that would be issued if all holders elected share settlement", "The SARs affect only the numerator through the remeasurement expense; no denominator adjustment is needed", "The SARs are included using the if-converted method because they are settled in a variable number of shares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22 and ASC 718-10-S99-2, share-settled SARs (and instruments where share settlement is possible) are included in diluted EPS via the treasury stock method regardless of liability classification. Incremental shares = 15,000 minus (15,000 x $20 / $60) = 10,000. The liability remeasurement expense is already in the numerator. Choice A is wrong because liability classification under ASC 718 does not exempt an instrument from diluted EPS when shares may be delivered.', 'hard', 3)
;

COMMIT;
